      "facts": [
        "The Monteverde and related Roman Jewish inscriptions document Greek and Latin names, family relations, symbols, and communal titles mainly in the third and fourth centuries.",
        "The inscriptions confirm long-term Jewish plurality and adaptation at Rome while preventing later titles, language ratios, or burial customs from being treated as direct facts about Romans' first recipients.",
        "Philo's near-contemporary account describes Jewish residents across the Tiber, many with citizenship after manumission, synagogues, Sabbath study, and contributions sent to Jerusalem.",
        "The evidence gives concrete Jewish-diaspora context for Romans and Acts 28 without making Roman Jews a uniform body or treating later catacomb offices as first-century institutions."
